>> title: Tonkotsu Broth
>> category: Soups
>> tags: pork, japanese, stock, ramen, stovetop
>> counters: Ramen Shop
>> aka: tonkotsu, tonkotsu broth, 豚骨, とんこつ, pork bone broth, white pork broth, Hakata broth, ramen broth, milky pork broth
>> pairs-with: tonkotsu-ramen
>> servings: 8
>> time: 9 hr 30 min
>> slack: unforgiving — let it drop from a hard rolling boil to a simmer and the fat separates back out, and the broth is grey for good

>> step: The one-off home version: 8 hours, one pot, and genuinely white.
A shop boils this twelve to eighteen hours and never empties the pot — it tops up, draws off, and starts again.

>> step: parboil 30 min, then scrub every bone
Cover @pork trotters{2%lb}(900 g; split lengthways by the butcher), @pork neck bones{2%lb}(900 g) and @pork fatback{6%oz}(170 g) with @cold water{5%qt}(4.7 L) in a #stockpot{}, bring to a hard boil and ~parboil{30%min}. Scrub every bone until nothing brown or grey clings to it, and scrub the pot.

>> step: boil hard 8 hr, topping the water up
Return @&(~1)scrubbed bones{} to the pot with @water{6%qt}(5.7 L) and ~boil{8%hr} at a hard rolling boil, topping the water back to its line every hour. Never let it fall to a simmer.

>> step: stir the aromatics in, last hour
Drop @yellow onion{1%large}(halved), @garlic{1%head}(halved across), @fresh ginger{2%in}(sliced) and @scallion greens{4%stalks} into @&(~1)broth{} for the last hour only, ~boil{1%hr}.

>> step: strain, pressing the softened marrow through
Strain @&(~1)broth{} through a coarse sieve, pressing the marrow and cartilage through with a ladle. It should coat a spoon hot and set to a firm jelly cold.
